    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Before buying any hair supplement, ask for five blood tests: a complete blood count, serum ferritin, TSH, 25-hydroxy vitamin D and vitamin B12.</strong> Pooled across 36 studies, women with non-scarring hair loss had ferritin about 18.5 ng/dL lower than women without it. Testing tells you whether a supplement is even the right purchase.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Ferritin first:</strong> it measures stored iron, and it can be low while haemoglobin still reads normal, so a plain blood count on its own can miss it.</li>
        <li><strong>Vitamin D:</strong> across 23 studies in 3,374 patients and 7,296 controls, the odds of deficiency were 3.11 times higher in non-scarring hair loss (95% CI 2.29 to 4.22).</li>
        <li><strong>Stop biotin first:</strong> the US Food and Drug Administration has warned that high-dose biotin skews immunoassays including TSH and cardiac troponin, in either direction.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> a low result explains an association, not a cure. No randomised trial in this research showed that correcting ferritin or vitamin D regrows hair.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

