      <h2 id="which-tests">Which blood tests should you get for hair fall?</h2>
      <p>Five tests cover almost all of the treatable nutritional and hormonal causes of diffuse shedding: a complete blood count, serum ferritin, TSH, 25-hydroxy vitamin D and vitamin B12. An Indian expert consensus on the management of telogen effluvium lists a complete blood count, routine urine examination, vitamin D and thyroid function tests among the basic investigations for diffuse hair loss.</p>
      <p>Two practical points decide whether the panel is useful. Ask for serum ferritin by name, because a complete blood count does not include it and iron stores can be depleted while haemoglobin is still inside the reference range. And resist the full-body package: a retrospective review of 2,851 women with telogen effluvium concluded that biochemical tests, complete blood counts and hormonal tests are the important tools here, which is a short list, not a 90-parameter one.</p>

