      <h2 id="ferritin">Does low ferritin really cause hair fall?</h2>
      <p>Low iron stores are strongly associated with diffuse hair loss in women, but the association is narrower than the supplement aisle implies, and it is not the same as frank anaemia.</p>
      <p>The best pooled estimate comes from Treister-Goltzman, Yarza and Peleg in <em>Skin Appendage Disorders</em> in 2022. They screened 928 records, included 36 studies in a random-effects meta-analysis, and found ferritin significantly lower in women with non-scarring hair loss, with a mean difference of -18.51 ng/dL (95% CI -25.85 to -11.16, p&lt;0.01). The same review reported that the rate of iron-deficiency <em>anaemia</em> among women with hair loss was not higher than in women generally.</p>
      <p>That distinction is the useful part. Iron stores run lower; the full-blown anaemia does not appear more often. It is also why ferritin has to be requested separately, and why a normal haemoglobin is not a reason to stop looking.</p>
      <p>An earlier retrospective cross-sectional study of patients with telogen effluvium, published in the <em>Journal of Drugs in Dermatology</em> in 2016, found the largest share of deficiencies in ferritin at 45.2%, followed by vitamin D at 33.9% and zinc at 9.6%. It had no control group, so it describes who turns up in a clinic rather than what causes shedding.</p>
      <p>The inconvenient half of the literature deserves equal space. A cross-sectional study of 40 Indian women aged 18 to 45 with diffuse hair loss against 40 matched controls, published in the <em>Indian Journal of Dermatopathology and Diagnostic Dermatology</em> in 2022, concluded that diffuse hair loss was associated with low ferritin and low vitamin D and showed no significant link with thyroid abnormality. Within that paper, the figure most often misquoted elsewhere, p=0.027, is a comparison of haemoglobin between the groups split by ferritin above and below 10 ng/mL (11.65 g% against 11 g%) rather than a hair loss result. And a 2024 case-control study in the <em>Journal of Cosmetic Dermatology</em> comparing 90 women with chronic telogen effluvium against 90 controls found no difference in haemoglobin, ferritin, B12, thyroid function, vitamin D, copper, or serum and urine biotin. Earlier work by Sinclair, cited in the 2022 Indian paper, had already argued there is no clear association between low ferritin and chronic diffuse telogen hair loss.</p>
      <p>So: test it, because it is cheap and sometimes it is the answer. Do not assume it is the answer.</p>

      <h2 id="vitamin-d">Is vitamin D deficiency behind my hair fall?</h2>
      <p>Vitamin D deficiency is clearly over-represented in people with hair loss. Whether it is a cause, and whether correcting it helps, are separate questions that have not been settled.</p>
      <p>The strongest pooled figure comes from Chen and colleagues in the <em>Journal of Cosmetic Dermatology</em> in 2024: 23 studies, 3,374 patients with non-scarring alopecia and 7,296 healthy controls. Serum 25-hydroxy vitamin D was lower in patients (weighted mean difference -7.29, 95% CI -9.21 to -5.38) and the odds of outright deficiency were 3.11 times higher (95% CI 2.29 to 4.22). Heterogeneity was high for the serum level (I&sup2; 95%) but zero for the deficiency outcome, which makes the deficiency finding the sturdier of the two.</p>
      <p>A 2024 review in <em>Frontiers in Nutrition</em> covering alopecia areata, androgenetic alopecia, telogen effluvium and primary cicatricial alopecia reached a measured conclusion: screening for vitamin D may benefit patients with alopecia areata and female pattern hair loss, and supplementation as a treatment needs further study. That is a recommendation to test, not a recommendation to dose.</p>
      <p>This matters more in India than the global literature suggests, because baseline vitamin D status here is poor and the deficiency case is met more often than in the trial populations. What a low result does and does not mean, and the ng/mL thresholds Indian labs use, is covered in <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">vitamin D deficiency symptoms</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="thyroid-zinc">What about thyroid, zinc and B12?</h2>
      <p>All three are reasonable to include, and none of them is the sure thing that Indian hair-fall content usually claims.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Thyroid.</strong> Thyroid disease genuinely causes diffuse shedding, so TSH belongs on the form, and it is most informative when there are other symptoms: fatigue, weight change, cold or heat intolerance, constipation, palpitations or menstrual change. But the most repeated claim in Indian hair-fall articles, that thyroid trouble is behind most hair fall, is not what the controlled studies found. Both the 2022 Indian study and the 2024 case-control study found no significant thyroid difference against controls.</li>
        <li><strong>Zinc.</strong> This was the one positive signal in the 2024 case-control study: zinc was significantly lower in women with chronic telogen effluvium than in controls, while selenium was significantly <em>higher</em> in patients. Two findings in opposite directions from one small female-only sample is a reason to measure zinc, not a reason to supplement it blind.</li>
        <li><strong>Vitamin B12.</strong> Worth including on a predominantly vegetarian diet, which is the ordinary case in India. The 2024 case-control study found no difference in B12 between patients and controls, so treat it as a general nutritional check rather than a hair test.</li>
      </ol>

