      <h2 id="biotin">Why you should stop biotin before the blood draw</h2>
      <p>If you are already taking a high-dose biotin hair supplement, tell the laboratory before the sample is taken. Biotin is used as part of many immunoassays, and at megadose levels it can push results away from the truth.</p>
      <p>The US Food and Drug Administration has issued a safety communication on this. Biotin features in immunoassays for cardiac troponin and thyroid stimulating hormone among many others, and biotin in the patient's serum can produce falsely high or falsely low results depending on the assay. The FDA has received a report of one patient death following falsely low troponin results while taking high levels of biotin. The agency notes that the recommended daily allowance, 0.03mg, does not typically interfere; the problem is the milligram doses sold for hair and nails.</p>
      <p>One detail is worth getting right, because consumer sites get it wrong. The common instruction to stop biotin 48 to 72 hours beforehand is prudence rather than a tested threshold. The FDA's own position is that sufficient information is not available to know whether stopping biotin for any particular number of hours prevents incorrect results. So the reliable step is disclosure, not a countdown.</p>
      <p>For reference, a Collabloom shot contains 45mcg of biotin, which is a co-factor amount close to the daily allowance and nowhere near the 5mg-and-above doses where interference is described. The hair gummies sold at 5,000 or 10,000mcg are the ones this warning is about.</p>

      <h2 id="will-it-help">If a test comes back low, will correcting it regrow hair?</h2>
      <p>Possibly, and the honest answer is that nobody has proved it. This research did not surface a randomised trial showing that correcting low ferritin or low vitamin D regrows hair.</p>
      <p>What exists is observational: deficiencies are more common in people who are shedding, and reviews describe the supplementation trials as small and inconclusive, with contradictory results in female pattern hair loss and telogen effluvium. A 2026 systematic review on diet and hair health reports improvement in hair growth with iron supplementation, but it pools observational work alongside trials, which makes the conclusion weaker than the sentence sounds.</p>
      <p>Two things follow for anyone holding a lab report. Do not start iron on your own. Supplementing without a confirmed deficiency risks accumulation, and a low ferritin result has causes worth finding rather than covering, including coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease and occult gastrointestinal bleeding. A raised ferritin is not self-explanatory either: a review of routine donor ferritin testing stresses that high ferritin has many non-iron causes and always needs further assessment. And give any correction time. Hair cycles slowly, and three to six months is the realistic window before judging a change, which is the same timescale described in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-hair">collagen for hair</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="see-a-doctor">When hair fall needs a doctor rather than a lab form</h2>
      <p>Some patterns of hair loss are not nutritional, and a few are permanent if left alone. These are reasons to be examined rather than tested first.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Patchy, coin-shaped loss.</strong> This suggests alopecia areata and needs a diagnosis, because scarring conditions can look similar early on.</li>
        <li><strong>Scalp pain, burning, tenderness, pustules, crusting or redness around individual hairs.</strong> These point to inflammation rather than shedding.</li>
        <li><strong>Smooth or shiny bald patches, or loss of the visible pores on the scalp.</strong> Loss of the follicular openings is one of the most important clues to scarring alopecia, where follicles are permanently destroyed and generally cannot produce hair again. Early assessment is the whole point.</li>
        <li><strong>A receding frontal hairline or thinning eyebrows</strong>, particularly in women.</li>
        <li><strong>A widening parting over months or years.</strong> That is pattern hair loss, and it needs prescription treatment rather than a supplement.</li>
        <li><strong>Hair loss with fatigue, weight change, heavy periods or new illness.</strong> Treat the hair as a symptom and get the rest looked at.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A dermatologist can examine the scalp with a dermatoscope and may take a biopsy where a scarring alopecia is suspected. Blood tests support that examination; they do not replace it.</p>

