      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Which blood tests should I get for hair fall?</summary>
          <p>A complete blood count, serum ferritin, TSH, 25-hydroxy vitamin D and vitamin B12 cover most treatable nutritional and hormonal causes, and serum zinc is a reasonable addition. An Indian expert consensus on telogen effluvium lists a complete blood count, routine urine examination, vitamin D and thyroid function tests among the basic investigations for diffuse hair loss. Ask your doctor which of these fits your pattern of loss.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can my haemoglobin be normal and iron still be causing hair fall?</summary>
          <p>Yes, which is the main reason to ask for ferritin by name. Ferritin measures stored iron and can be depleted while haemoglobin is still within the reference range. A 2022 meta-analysis of 36 studies found ferritin 18.51 ng/dL lower in women with non-scarring hair loss, while reporting that iron-deficiency anaemia itself was no more common than in women generally.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need to stop biotin before a blood test?</summary>
          <p>Tell the laboratory you take it, and ask them what they want you to do. The US Food and Drug Administration has warned that high-dose biotin can make immunoassays including TSH and cardiac troponin read falsely high or falsely low, and has received a report of one death after falsely low troponin results. The common advice to stop 48 to 72 hours beforehand is prudence rather than a tested threshold, as the FDA says there is not enough information to know whether stopping for any set number of hours prevents wrong results.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will taking iron fix my hair fall?</summary>
          <p>Not reliably, and not without a test first. This research found no randomised trial showing that correcting ferritin regrows hair, only observational associations. Supplementing without a confirmed deficiency risks accumulation, and a low ferritin has causes worth investigating in their own right, so iron should be started on a doctor's advice rather than bought off a shelf.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is vitamin D deficiency causing my hair loss?</summary>
          <p>It is strongly associated with it, though cause is not established. Across 23 studies covering 3,374 patients and 7,296 controls, the odds of vitamin D deficiency were 3.11 times higher in non-scarring alopecia (95% CI 2.29 to 4.22). A 2024 review suggested screening may benefit patients with alopecia areata and female pattern hair loss while saying supplementation as a treatment still needs study.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I book a full-body health package instead?</summary>
          <p>It is usually unnecessary. A retrospective review of 2,851 women with telogen effluvium concluded that blood counts, biochemical tests and hormonal tests are the useful tools, which is a short panel rather than a 90-parameter profile. The right tests depend on your pattern of loss and your symptoms, so a focused list agreed with a doctor beats an online bundle.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help hair fall that has a medical cause?</summary>
          <p>No. A Collabloom shot contains no iron, no vitamin D and no vitamin B12, so it cannot correct the deficiencies most often found in people who are shedding, and its 1.5mg zinc and 45mcg biotin are co-factor amounts rather than correction doses. Pattern hair loss, thyroid disease and scarring alopecias need medical treatment. Collagen supports dietary protein intake and skin quality, which is a different job.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long should I wait before deciding a test result explained anything?</summary>
          <p>Three to six months. Hair grows and cycles slowly, so any change in diet, a corrected deficiency or a prescription treatment needs at least that long before it can be judged fairly. Shorter trials of anything, supplements included, mostly measure impatience.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

